[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Although the author's change attempted to implement DAG, WaveScheduler, and DAGRunner with full graph, scheduling, and execution semantics, unreachable code branches remain in the implementation under the specification closure. Specifically, in DAG.remove_node the exception handler wrapping WaveScheduler node purging is unreachable because the registered singleton and purge operation cannot raise under the contract; in WaveScheduler._sync_states the loop deleting removed nodes is unreachable because DAG.remove_node already purges nodes from scheduler tracking; and in DAGRunner.run the break branch guarding an empty next wave is unreachable because is_finished guarantees a non-empty wave when unfinished. Please restructure the code to eliminate these dead branches or annotate them with # pragma: no cover (assumption: <reason>) for caller assumptions guaranteed by the contract.
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Although the author's change attempted to implement Pretense state machine, synthesized fakes, unit tests, real implementation tests, and fuzzing for dag_engine_lite_impl, coverage deficits remain across multiple specification postconditions and error branches. Specifically, test coverage lacks verification of: get_dependencies and get_dependents raising NodeNotFoundError when given an unmapped node identifier; topological_sort raising CycleError in the presence of directed cycles; compute_critical_path handling empty graphs; WaveScheduler.get_state raising NodeNotFoundError for nonexistent nodes; WaveScheduler.mark_failed applying FailurePolicy.SKIP_DOWNSTREAM and FailurePolicy.CONTINUE_ON_ERROR to transition blocked dependents to SKIPPED; WaveScheduler.is_finished evaluating graphs with running nodes or unexecuted nodes blocked by failed dependencies; and DAGRunner.run executing under failing node actions with retry attempts, reporting failed, skipped, and cancelled node outcomes under the active failure policy.
